  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Barry Trupin is an American financier known for structuring and selling tax-oriented investments through the Rothschild Reserve group in New York. His businesses offered computer-equipment leasing arrangements before expanding into property partnerships during the 1980s. These ventures generated the wealth that financed his purchases of art and substantial residential properties.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 88,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Business background and wealth mechanism are directly recorded in several judicial factual accounts."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Barry Trupin is an American financier known for structuring and selling tax-oriented investments through the Rothschild Reserve group in New York. His businesses offered computer-equipment leasing arrangements before expanding into property partnerships during the 1980s. These ventures generated the wealth that financed his purchases of art and substantial residential properties.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "New York financier Barry Trupin bought a former du Pont residence in Southampton and named it Dragon's Head. He enlarged the house, adding a cinema and an aquarium intended for sharks. The unfinished property was eventually sold to developer Francesco Galesi, bringing Trupin's ownership of the elaborate seaside project to an end.\n\nIn Manhattan, he acquired the former home of Lucy Drexel Dahlgren, designed by architect Ogden Codman Jr. The house had also belonged to jeweller Pierre Cartier before becoming a convent. Trupin had its electrical and plumbing systems rebuilt. It later passed to businessman Paul Singer, who undertook a more extensive restoration of the Beaux Arts building.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 88,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full Forbes property article and cited architectural history distinguish his works from subsequent owners' restorations; contemporaneous report confirms unfinished state."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S6",
      "S8"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>New York financier Barry Trupin bought a former du Pont residence in Southampton and named it Dragon's Head. He enlarged the house, adding a cinema and an aquarium intended for sharks. The unfinished property was eventually sold to developer Francesco Galesi, bringing Trupin's ownership of the elaborate seaside project to an end.</p>\r\n<p>In Manhattan, he acquired the former home of Lucy Drexel Dahlgren, designed by architect Ogden Codman Jr. The house had also belonged to jeweller Pierre Cartier before becoming a convent. Trupin had its electrical and plumbing systems rebuilt. It later passed to businessman Paul Singer, who undertook a more extensive restoration of the Beaux Arts building.</p>\r\n"
  },
